<h2>A timeline that would not add up</h2>
<p>On 29 September 2025, an enforcement team intercepted a truck in Tripura. The first seizure memo records the interception at 2:00 pm, when 11.03 kg of suspected methamphetamine tablets were seized; a second seizure at the Customs Division office, completed by 5:00 pm the same day, took the total to 60.79 kg — against a commercial quantity threshold of 50 grams. Yet the accused were &ldquo;formally&rdquo; arrested only at 11:00 and 11:15 the next morning, 30 September 2025, and produced before the magistrate at 4:45 pm that afternoon.</p>
<p>Counted from the 2:00 pm interception, production took more than twenty-six hours. The prosecution&rsquo;s answer was that the clock starts at formal arrest. The High Court accepted a distinction between &ldquo;detention&rdquo; for the purposes of search and investigation and &ldquo;arrest&rdquo; proper, and rejected bail on 29 April 2026, holding additionally that Section 37 of the NDPS Act barred release.</p>

<h2>What the Supreme Court held</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Detention is arrest.</strong> The Court held that the words &ldquo;arrested&rdquo; and &ldquo;detained&rdquo; in Article 22(2) are synonymous: &ldquo;the Constitution does not recognize any detention dehors arrest&rdquo; (para 58), and there is &ldquo;no such thing called temporary disruption of liberty&rdquo; (para 57).</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The deadline is sacrosanct.</strong> Only the journey time to the magistrate may be excluded: &ldquo;The Constitution does not permit any other time period to be excluded from the 24 hours period&rdquo; (para 16.1). Procedural compliance under the NDPS Act — search, sampling, seizure — does not pause the clock (para 40).</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Breach makes detention unconstitutional.</strong> The arrest was construed to have taken place at 2:00 pm on 29 September 2025; continued detention beyond 2:00 pm on 30 September was held &ldquo;illegal and unconstitutional&rdquo; (para 66). The unexplained 18-hour overnight gap between the end of the search and the formal arrest also fell foul of the reasonableness requirement of Section 58 BNSS (para 67).</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Bail follows.</strong> The appeals were allowed; the accused are to be produced before the jurisdictional Special Court within seven days and released on bail on appropriate terms (para 69(ix)).</div>
</div>

<h2>Three High Court decisions disapproved</h2>
<p>The Court expressly held that the Orissa High Court in <em>Aryaman Pattnayak</em>, the Madras High Court in <em>Kannan</em> and the Kerala High Court in <em>Arun K. Thomas</em> do not state the correct law on Article 22(2) (para 69(iv)). Each had, in one form or another, treated preliminary detention or statutory procedure as standing outside the constitutional clock. The Bench found the attempt &ldquo;to create an artificial distinction between detention and arrest&rdquo; to be &ldquo;totally unwarranted and highly problematic&rdquo; (para 37.2).</p>

<h2>Section 37 and the Constitution</h2>
<p>The respondent urged that whatever the procedural breach, Section 37 of the NDPS Act barred bail. The Court granted bail without any twin-conditions analysis, resting release squarely on the constitutional violation. It noted its reservations about <em>Sukhwinder Singh @ Gora</em> (2026), observing that fundamental rights &ldquo;cannot be tinkered with so lightly, not even under the special statutes&rdquo;, and that the view that Article 21 operates only within Section 37 runs counter to <em>K.A. Najeeb</em>, <em>Satender Kumar Antil</em>, <em>Mohd. Muslim</em> and allied decisions (para 49).</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Systemic directions:</strong> the magistrate must record the actual date and time of detention at first production and verify 24-hour compliance; a breach requires release forthwith, and even a within-time detention may justify declining remand if unreasonably long (paras 69(v)-(vii)). The judgment is to be circulated to all DGPs, Registrars General of the High Courts and State Judicial Academies (para 70).</p>
</div>

<h2>Why it matters in practice</h2>
<p>For bail practice across special statutes — NDPS, UAPA, PMLA alike — the decision arms the defence with a crisp factual enquiry: when, exactly, was liberty first curtailed, and what does the record show happened in the hours that followed? Seizure memos, CCTV, call records and the arrest memo&rsquo;s own timings now carry constitutional weight. For investigating agencies, the message is equally direct: the practice of holding a suspect overnight and recording a morning arrest invites not just criticism but release. Advocates defending accused persons in Delhi&rsquo;s special courts will find this judgment the natural starting point wherever the production timeline looks strained.</p>
